        Root cause:
        The bug was caused by the use of guest physical addresses that overlapped with a reserved address range defined by HyperTransport on AMD processors. This overlap could lead to failures during self-tests, particularly when the maximum guest frame number (gfn) was calculated without accounting for these reserved addresses, especially on processors with different physical address bit configurations and Secure Memory Encryption (SME) settings.

        Commit message:
        selftests: KVM: avoid failures due to reserved HyperTransport
 region

AMD proceessors define an address range that is reserved by HyperTransport
and causes a failure if used for guest physical addresses.  Avoid
selftests failures by reserving those guest physical addresses; the
rules are:

- On parts with <40 bits, its fully hidden from software.

- Before Fam17h, it was always 12G just below 1T, even if there was more
RAM above this location.  In this case we just not use any RAM above 1T.

- On Fam17h and later, it is variable based on SME, and is either just
below 2^48 (no encryption) or 2^43 (encryption).

